(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-cms-exam-3-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 3 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 44-year-old man develops unilateral hearing loss over two days with no identifiable cause after investigation. He asks what the diagnosis actually means. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["It indicates a conductive problem that surgery can correct","It is a syndrome rather than a disease, and a cause is often never found","It is always caused by a viral infection of the cochlea","It reliably resolves completely without treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sudden sensorineural hearing loss describes a pattern of presentation, not a single pathology, and in most cases no cause is identified despite investigation. The clinical importance is that the differential includes treatable and dangerous entries, which is why it prompts urgent referral and imaging rather than a wait to see what happens. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 56-year-old woman has noticed over 18 months that she struggles to use the telephone with her left ear, though she can hear the tone. An audiogram shows a mild left sensorineural loss, but her word recognition score on that side is far poorer than the pure tone thresholds predict. She also has mild unsteadiness. Which investigation is most appropriate?","choices":["Computed tomography of the temporal bones without contrast","Magnetic resonance imaging with gadolinium","Repeat audiometry in twelve months","Tympanometry"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Speech discrimination that is disproportionately poor for the pure tone loss points beyond the cochlea to the nerve itself, because a retrocochlear lesion degrades the signal's clarity more than its audibility. Gadolinium-enhanced magnetic resonance imaging is the gold standard for detecting a vestibular schwannoma in the internal auditory canal. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 38-year-old has had six months of nasal obstruction, a reduced sense of smell and clear rhinorrhea. Nasal examination shows multiple pale, insensate, grape-like masses in both nasal cavities. The clinician plans to screen for the conditions that accompany this finding. What are the recognized associations of this finding?","choices":["Allergic fungal sinusitis, asthma and cystic fibrosis","Asthma is associated in under 5% of cases","Cystic fibrosis is associated in over 90% of cases","No systemic conditions are associated with nasal polyps"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All three accompany multiple benign polyps: polyps occur in about 85% of patients with allergic fungal sinusitis and in 6 to 44% of patients with cystic fibrosis, and asthma is present in 20 to 50% of patients with polyps. Aspirin intolerance (8 to 26% of polyp patients), alcohol intolerance (50%) and Churg-Strauss syndrome (polyps in 50%) also belong on the list. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 3-year-old girl has had two days of fever and left ear pain following a cold. Otoscopy shows a bulging, opaque tympanic membrane with reduced mobility on pneumatic otoscopy. She has had no previous ear infections and no drug allergies. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["High-dose oral amoxicillin","Oral azithromycin","Reassurance and analgesia alone, with no antibiotic","Topical ciprofloxacin drops"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A bulging drum with reduced mobility and acute fever indicates pus under pressure in the middle ear, which is bacterial acute otitis media rather than a simple effusion. Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis are the organisms, and high-dose amoxicillin is first line in a child with no recent antibiotic exposure and no penicillin allergy. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 52-year-old has noticed reduced hearing in the right ear over the past year, with no pain, discharge or vertigo. On tuning fork testing with a 512 hertz fork, the Weber lateralizes to the right ear, and on the right bone conduction is heard longer than air conduction. What do these findings indicate?","choices":["A conductive loss in the left ear","A conductive loss in the right ear","A sensorineural loss in the right ear","Normal hearing in both ears"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two findings agree. Weber lateralizing toward an ear means that ear hears bone-conducted sound better, which happens when a conductive block shields it from competing ambient noise. A reversed Rinne in the same ear, with bone beating air, means sound is being obstructed before it reaches a working cochlea. Both point to the conducting mechanism. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has a septal perforation. She uses no intranasal medication and denies drug use. The clinician considers systemic causes. Which causes should be considered?","choices":["Allergic rhinitis","Chronic sinusitis","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis, or syphilis","Nasal polyposis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When the two common local causes, intranasal corticosteroid and cocaine, are excluded, granulomatosis with polyangiitis and secondary syphilis as the rarer systemic causes. Both destroy the septal mucosa and cartilage through inflammation rather than local ischemia, and both need systemic treatment. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man with vestibular neuronitis asks how long the vertigo will last and what he should do. What should the patient be told?","choices":["It is permanent and requires surgical treatment","It settles over days to weeks; move about rather than lie still","Remain on strict bed rest until symptoms resolve completely","Take vestibular suppressants continuously for several weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The condition is benign and self-limiting, and recovery depends on central compensation, which is driven by vestibular activity. Prolonged bed rest and continued vestibular suppressants both delay that process, so early mobilization is what shortens the illness. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl is struck in the nose during a hockey match. She has swelling and tenderness over the bridge. Examination of the nasal cavity shows bilateral bluish, boggy swelling of the septum that is soft to touch. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Drain it now","Ice, analgesia and review in one week","Nasal packing to compress the swelling","Radiography to exclude a nasal fracture first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blood collecting between the septal cartilage and its perichondrium strips the cartilage of its only blood supply, and the cartilage necroses within days, producing a saddle nose deformity and septal perforation. It can also abscess. Drainage through an intranasal incision is therefore urgent rather than elective. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with asthma has nasal polyps and a history of severe bronchospasm after taking aspirin. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Allergic fungal sinusitis","Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease","Churg-Strauss syndrome","Cystic fibrosis presenting in adulthood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The triad of asthma, nasal polyposis and aspirin sensitivity reflects abnormal arachidonic acid handling: blocking cyclo-oxygenase shunts the pathway toward leukotriene production, which drives both the bronchospasm and the polyp formation. Recognizing it matters because all non-steroidal anti-inflammatories must be avoided. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has painless white lesions on the lateral border of the tongue that wax and wane. He is otherwise well and has no known medical conditions. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Excisional biopsy to exclude carcinoma","Prescribe topical antifungal therapy","Reassure and discharge, as it is benign","Test for HIV"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hairy leukoplakia is a benign mucosal hyperplasia driven by Epstein-Barr virus, and it is strongly associated with HIV, where it is a common early finding. The lesion itself needs little more than observation, so its clinical value lies almost entirely in what it points to in a patient with no known diagnosis. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 136"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has had chronic unilateral parotid swelling with minimal pain for several months. He has risk factors for tuberculosis. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute suppurative sialadenitis","Chronic granulomatous sialadenitis","Mumps parotitis","Sialolithiasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic unilateral or bilateral gland swelling with minimal pain is the picture of granulomatous sialadenitis, and primary tuberculosis should be considered when risk factors are present. The differential is wide, including cat scratch disease, sarcoidosis, actinomycosis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis and syphilis, so aspiration biopsy is needed. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman has pulsatile tinnitus and progressive conductive hearing loss in the left ear over a year. Otoscopy shows a reddish-blue mass behind the lower part of the tympanic membrane that blanches with pneumatic pressure. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cholesteatoma","Glomus tumor","Haemotympanum","Otitis media with effusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A glomus tumor is a highly vascular paraganglioma arising in the middle ear or jugular bulb. Its blood flow is what produces pulsatile tinnitus, its mass effect on the ossicular chain produces the conductive loss, and its vascularity is what makes it appear red-blue behind the drum and blanch when pressure is applied. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 6-year-old with suspected epiglottitis is transferred to theater. A student asks why the incidence of this disease has fallen so sharply. What is the explanation?","choices":["Haemophilus influenzae type B vaccination","Improved nutrition and living conditions","Pneumococcal conjugate vaccination","Widespread use of antibiotics for sore throat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The commonest pathogen in the pediatric population is Haemophilus influenzae type B, and routine immunization has reduced the incidence of supraglottitis by over 90 percent. It still occurs, from Streptococcus pneumoniae, Staphylococcus aureus and beta-hemolytic streptococci and in unimmunized children, which is why awareness still matters. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 3-year-old has a unilateral neck mass in the anterior triangle with overlying brawny, reddish-brown skin, induration and mild tenderness. She is systemically well and afebrile. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atypical mycobacterial infection","Cat scratch disease","Suppurative bacterial lymphadenitis","Tuberculous adenitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Non-tuberculous mycobacterial adenitis is a pediatric disease that presents as a unilateral mass in the anterior triangle or parotid region with characteristic brawny reddish-brown skin discoloration over it, in a child who is otherwise well. Diagnosis is by acid-fast stain or culture with skin testing, and treatment is usually surgical excision. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 26-year-old has had discharge from the left ear for four days with mild discomfort. The clinician notes the discharge is scanty rather than profuse, and is trying to work out whether the problem lies in the canal or behind the drum, which cannot be seen clearly. How does the character of the discharge help localize it?","choices":["Bloody discharge always indicates malignancy","Discharge character carries no localizing information","Foul-smelling discharge always indicates cholesteatoma","Scanty suggests canal, mucoid suggests middle ear"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The canal has no mucous glands, so discharge arising there is scanty, often itchy, and made of desquamated skin and exudate. Mucoid discharge implies mucous membrane, which exists in the middle ear, so profuse mucoid otorrhea indicates it is coming through a perforation rather than from the canal itself. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy has had acute otitis media for a week. He now has fever, tenderness over the mastoid process, and the left auricle is displaced downward and outward. There is swelling and erythema behind the ear. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute mastoiditis","Otitis externa with periauricular cellulitis","Parotitis","Preauricular lymphadenitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The mastoid air cells connect directly with the middle ear, so untreated or inadequately treated otitis media can extend into them. Postauricular erythema, swelling and tenderness with an auricle pushed down and out is the described picture, and it means the infection has moved from a mucosal space into bone, which is why it needs admission and intravenous therapy. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man develops sudden severe vertigo lasting several days after a viral illness, accompanied by reduced hearing and tinnitus in the left ear. There are no other neurological findings. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","Labyrinthitis","Meniere disease","Vestibular neuronitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Inflammation of the labyrinth involves both the vestibular and the cochlear divisions, so the patient gets sustained vertigo lasting days to weeks TOGETHER WITH hearing loss and tinnitus. The presence of auditory symptoms alongside the vertigo is the single feature that distinguishes it from vestibular neuronitis. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 91"},{"q":"A 13-year-old girl has an earring back embedded in the soft tissue of the earlobe, with surrounding erythema and tenderness. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Apply topical steroid to reduce the inflammation","Leave it in situ and treat with oral antibiotics","Refer routinely to a plastic surgeon","Remove it, because infection is the main risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An embedded earring back is a retained foreign body sitting in soft tissue that has closed over it, and the described erythema and tenderness show infection is already established. Removal is both the treatment of the infection's source and the prevention of spread into the auricular cartilage, where perichondritis can deform the ear. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman on chemotherapy has facial pain and fever with sinus opacification. Her clinician notes that the usual approach may need to change. How should her management differ?","choices":["Antibiotics should be withheld until cultures return","She can be managed identically to an immunocompetent patient","She needs earlier imaging and a lower threshold for invasive fungal disease","Topical decongestants alone are sufficient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Immunosuppression removes the neutrophil response that normally contains fungal hyphae, so Aspergillus and the mucormycetes can invade blood vessels and cause tissue infarction within days. That is why the usual watchful approach to sinusitis is abandoned and why the nasal mucosa is inspected specifically for necrotic tissue. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man who smokes and drinks daily has a firm, non-tender neck mass that has been present for six weeks and measures 3 centimeters. It is immobile and there is no history of infection. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["A two-week course of antibiotics and reassessment","Excisional biopsy of the node","Fine needle aspiration biopsy","Reassurance, as most neck masses are benign"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Every red flag is present: no infectious origin, duration over two weeks, size over 1.5 centimeters, firm and non-tender with little mobility, age over 40, and tobacco with alcohol. Fine needle aspiration is the standard of care because it separates neoplasm from inflammation and carcinoma from lymphoma without spilling tumor. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 63-year-old has had eight months of unsteadiness without discrete attacks of spinning. Hearing is normal and there are no neurological deficits. The clinician wants to know whether one labyrinth is weaker than the other, which requires testing each vestibular apparatus separately. Which investigation is most appropriate?","choices":["Dix-Hallpike maneuver","Electronystagmography","Epley maneuver","Pure tone audiometry"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Electronystagmography records eye movements during caloric irrigation, which allows each labyrinth to be stimulated on its own. That is what makes it the gold standard for vestibular assessment: most bedside tests examine the system as a whole, whereas this one can say which side is underperforming. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man comes to the emergency department with a nosebleed lasting over two hours. There is no trauma and no bleeding disorder. He is holding a blood-soaked towel; there is brisk bleeding from both nostrils and the site cannot be seen. He feels dizzy and appears confused. What should be done first?","choices":["Burn the bleeding vessels with cautery","Pinch the nose","Spray a topical anesthetic and decongestant","Start an intravenous line and give fluids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dizziness and confusion in someone who has bled briskly for two hours are signs of hemodynamic compromise, and resuscitation precedes definitive control of the bleeding point. Bleeding from both nostrils with no visible anterior source suggests a posterior bleed from the sphenopalatine artery, which is the type that causes significant blood loss. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 40"},{"q":"An 8-year-old recently arrived from a region with low immunization coverage has a mild sore throat, low-grade fever and marked malaise. Examination shows a tenacious gray membrane covering the tonsils and pharynx that bleeds when disturbed. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Corticosteroids to reduce the pharyngeal swelling","Diphtheria antitoxin plus antibiotics","Penicillin alone for 10 days","Supportive care, as it is likely viral pharyngitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A tenacious adherent gray membrane in an unimmunized child is diphtheria. The organism's damage is caused by an exotoxin that produces myocarditis and a cranial neuropathy, so antitoxin is required to neutralize circulating toxin, alongside penicillin or erythromycin for 14 days and isolation until three consecutive cultures are negative. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman develops sudden severe vertigo with nausea and vomiting two weeks after a viral upper respiratory illness. It is constant rather than positional, has lasted three days, and is worse on head movement. Her hearing is unchanged and there are no other neurological signs. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","Cerebellar infarction","Labyrinthitis","Vestibular neuronitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inflammation of the vestibular nerve alone, typically post-viral, gives dramatic sustained vertigo over days with nausea and vomiting. The critical discriminator is that hearing is completely spared, because the cochlear division is not involved. It is benign and self-limiting, settling over days to weeks as central compensation occurs. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 19-year-old has a nosebleed that started spontaneously twenty minutes ago and is still bleeding. There is no trauma, no anticoagulant use and no bleeding history. The bleeding appears to be coming from the front of the septum. What first aid instruction should be given?","choices":["Apply pressure for thirty seconds at a time, releasing to check","Pack the nose immediately with gauze at home","Pinch the bony bridge of the nose and tilt the head back","Pinch the soft part for ten minutes, leaning forward"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The bleeding point in 90 percent of cases is Kiesselbach plexus on the anterior septum, which is compressed by pinching the alae rather than the bony bridge. Continuous pressure matters because releasing to check disrupts the forming clot, and leaning forward stops blood tracking backward into the pharynx where it is swallowed or aspirated. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 4-year-old has had reduced hearing for six weeks following a cold. There is no pain or fever. Otoscopy shows a dull, retracted drum with an amber tinge and no erythema. The clinician performs pneumatic otoscopy to confirm a middle ear effusion. Which finding would confirm it?","choices":["A bulging erythematous drum","A visible cone of light","Increased mobility of the drum","Reduced mobility of the drum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pneumatic otoscopy applies a small pressure change and watches the drum move. Fluid behind it damps that movement, so reduced mobility is the finding that confirms an effusion and distinguishes it from a drum that merely looks dull. It is the bedside equivalent of a flat tympanogram. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with a tongue lesion reports pain in her right ear, though examination of the ear is entirely normal. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Coincidental otitis media","Eustachian tube dysfunction","Referred otalgia from cranial nerve involvement","Temporomandibular joint dysfunction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The tongue and oropharynx share sensory innervation with the ear through branches of the trigeminal, glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves, so a tumor irritating those nerves is perceived as ear pain. A normal ear examination in a patient with an oral lesion should raise this rather than reassure, because it signals advanced disease. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 146"},{"q":"A 3-month-old breastfed infant has white plaques on the tongue and buccal mucosa that wipe off. He is otherwise thriving. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Investigate urgently for immunodeficiency","Nystatin oral suspension","Oral fluconazole","Topical corticosteroid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thrush is common in infants, whose immune systems and oral flora are still establishing, and topical antifungal therapy is the appropriate first step. Nystatin suspension acts locally with negligible systemic absorption, which is the right profile for a well infant with a superficial infection. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 90"},{"q":"A 44-year-old had a total thyroidectomy and central neck dissection for medullary thyroid carcinoma eight months ago. There is no palpable recurrence. The clinician is arranging the surveillance blood test that tracks residual or recurrent disease in this particular tumor. Which blood test is it?","choices":["Calcitonin","Parathyroid hormone","Thyroglobulin","Thyroid-stimulating hormone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Medullary carcinoma arises from the parafollicular C cells, whose normal product is calcitonin, so the tumor secretes it and the level tracks tumor burden. That makes calcitonin a specific marker for detecting residual or recurrent disease, which is why screening labs form part of follow-up alongside thyroidectomy. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 32-year-old diver has ear pain and vertigo on ascent, with hearing loss that has not settled after several hours. What should be suspected?","choices":["Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo triggered by the dive","Decompression sickness affecting the vestibular system","Simple middle ear barotrauma requiring only decongestants","Suspect inner ear barotrauma with a possible perilymphatic fistula"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Middle ear barotrauma alone causes pain and conductive loss that settle as the effusion clears. Persistent vertigo and sensorineural loss after diving suggest the pressure change has ruptured a round or oval window membrane, letting perilymph leak, which needs prompt specialist assessment and avoidance of straining. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 55-year-old reports ringing in the ears for the past four months. It is present in both ears, constant, and not in time with the pulse. Hearing is normal on audiometry and examination is unremarkable. The clinician is deciding whether imaging is warranted. Which features of tinnitus require investigation?","choices":["High-pitched or low-pitched quality","Present for more than six months","Unilateral or pulsatile","Worse at night"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most tinnitus is a bilateral non-pulsatile ringing accompanying symmetrical hearing loss and needs no imaging. Unilaterality raises the possibility of an asymmetric lesion such as a schwannoma, and pulsatility suggests turbulent blood flow or a vascular middle ear mass such as a glomus tumor. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man has fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss with episodic vertigo and tinnitus, clinically indistinguishable from Meniere disease. His clinician orders serologic testing before settling on the diagnosis. What is being looked for, and why?","choices":["Acoustic neuroma","Autoimmune inner ear disease","Ototoxicity","Syphilis, which mimics Meniere"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Otosyphilis can reproduce the Meniere picture exactly, and the reason it is worth excluding is that it has a specific curative treatment while Meniere does not. Missing it means committing a patient to symptomatic management of an incurable syndrome when the underlying infection could have been eradicated. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man who has chewed tobacco for thirty years has a white plaque on the lateral border of the tongue. It cannot be scraped off and has been present for four months. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Excisional biopsy","Reassurance, since white oral lesions are usually benign","Topical antifungal therapy and reassessment in two weeks","Topical corticosteroid for presumed lichen planus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Leukoplakia is a premalignant lesion by definition, with 5 to 20 percent progressing to squamous cell carcinoma, and clinical appearance cannot tell which lesions harbor dysplasia or invasion. Biopsy is therefore the diagnostic step, alongside eliminating the carcinogenic irritant and a complete intraoral examination with palpation for lymphadenopathy. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 134"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has facial pain, clicking of the jaw and difficulty opening her mouth wide. She also reports tinnitus, a sensation of ear fullness and occasional dizziness. Otoscopy and audiometry are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acoustic neuroma","Eustachian tube dysfunction","Meniere disease","Temporomandibular joint disorder"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The temporomandibular joint sits immediately anterior to the ear canal, so its disorders commonly produce referred ear symptoms including tinnitus, fullness and dizziness with a completely normal ear examination. Jaw pain with clicking and limited opening in a woman of childbearing age is the typical presentation, and it is the second commonest musculoskeletal cause of pain and disability. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 130"},{"q":"A 4-year-old has had a bilateral middle ear effusion documented for four months with a measurable conductive hearing loss. Speech development has slowed. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["A course of oral antibiotics","Continue observation for a further three months","Oral antihistamines and decongestants","Refer for tympanostomy tubes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The threshold for intervention in a persistent effusion is roughly three months with documented hearing loss, and this child is beyond it with a functional consequence in speech. Tubes ventilate the middle ear and restore hearing during the period when language is being acquired, which is the argument for acting rather than continuing to wait. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 47-year-old is having a nosebleed and there are visible clots in both nostrils. The patient has been holding a tissue to the nose for ten minutes without pressure and asks whether it is safe to blow the nose before pinching it. What should the patient be told?","choices":["Blow out clots first, then apply pressure and a vasoconstrictor","Blow the nose repeatedly throughout the pressure period","Never blow the nose at any point","Tilt the head back so blood does not stain clothing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Retained clot holds the vessel open and prevents the mucosal edges from apposing, so clearing it lets a fresh clot form against a compressed vessel. The sequence is to blow out clots, spray a topical vasoconstrictor such as oxymetazoline, then pinch the alae continuously for ten minutes. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has recurrent painful swelling under the right side of his jaw that comes on within minutes of starting a meal and settles over the following hour. On examination a firm structure is palpable in the floor of the mouth. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A parotid neoplasm","Acute suppurative sialadenitis","Mumps parotitis","Submandibular sialolithiasis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Eating stimulates salivary flow, and a stone obstructing the duct traps that saliva behind it, distending the gland until the pressure equalizes. That is why the pain is timed to meals and settles afterwards, and why it is called salivary colic. Between 80 and 90 percent of stones are submandibular because the duct runs a long uphill course carrying alkaline, mucin-rich saliva. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 29-year-old has a stab wound in the supraclavicular triangle at the root of the neck. The patient is short of breath and oxygen saturation is falling. The surgeon is most concerned about a structure lying deep to the contents of that triangle. Which structure is it?","choices":["The cupola of the pleural cavity","The spinal accessory nerve","The thoracic duct on the right side","The vagus nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The supraclavicular triangle lies above the middle of the clavicle and contains the terminal subclavian artery and the roots, trunks and divisions of the brachial plexus, but the structure lying DEEP to all of them is the dome of the pleura. A wound there can therefore produce a pneumothorax as well as vascular and neurological injury. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 9-year-old has a slow-growing, asymptomatic lower anterior neck mass. Biopsy shows Hassall corpuscles. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Branchial cleft cyst","Dermoid cyst","Thymic cyst","Thyroglossal duct cyst"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hassall corpuscles are concentric epithelial structures found in the thymic medulla, and their presence is what makes the diagnosis definitively. Imaging narrows the differential but cannot establish it, which is why definitive diagnosis is by biopsy. Treatment is surgical excision. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 16-year-old has a four-day history of sore throat and fever. The right palatine tonsil is enlarged and covered in exudate, and there is a tender enlarged node in the upper deep cervical chain. Which group of deep cervical nodes drains this tonsil?","choices":["The jugulodigastric nodes","The juguloomohyoid nodes","The occipital nodes","The submandibular nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The deep cervical chain receives drainage from all the regional node groups, and two members of it have named territories. The jugulodigastric node drains the palatine tonsil, which is why tonsillitis characteristically produces a tender node high in the jugular chain just below the angle of the jaw. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man descends on a flight with a heavy cold. He develops severe right ear pain during descent, followed by muffled hearing. Otoscopy shows a dark blue-black discoloration behind an intact tympanic membrane. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute otitis media","Cholesteatoma","Glomus tumor","Hemotympanum from barotrauma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A blocked eustachian tube cannot equalize the rising ambient pressure during descent, so relative negative pressure in the middle ear pulls fluid and then blood out of the mucosal vessels. The blood behind an intact drum gives the blue-black appearance, and the conductive loss follows from fluid rather than from any damage to the cochlea. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has clear bilateral rhinorrhea triggered by cold air, strong smells and alcohol. He has no itch, no sneezing paroxysms and negative allergy testing. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Allergic rhinitis","Cerebrospinal fluid rhinorrhea","Chronic bacterial sinusitis","Vasomotor rhinitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vasomotor rhinitis produces clear bilateral discharge like allergic rhinitis, which is why they are considered together, but it is driven by autonomic dysregulation rather than an immunoglobulin E response. The triggers are physical and chemical rather than allergenic, and itch and ocular symptoms are characteristically absent. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 64-year-old smoker has a firm, fixed 4 cm cervical node that has grown over three months. There is no primary tumor visible on examination of the mouth or throat. The clinician arranges fine needle aspiration rather than excisional biopsy. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Aspiration can be repeated whereas excision cannot","Aspiration is more accurate than excision","Excision requires general anesthesia and aspiration does not","Excision spills tumor and complicates definitive treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Opening a malignant node releases cells into the surrounding tissue planes, which seeds the neck outside the original nodal compartment and makes both the surgical field and the radiotherapy field harder to define. Aspiration samples the node without breaching its capsule. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with allergic rhinitis has recurrent ear fullness and popping. A student asks why allergy affects the ear. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Allergens reach the middle ear directly and inflame it","Histamine damages the cochlear hair cells","Increased cerumen production blocks the canal","Mucosal edema obstructs the eustachian tube"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The eustachian tube is lined by the same respiratory mucosa as the nose, so allergic inflammation swells it and closes the lumen. Once the tube cannot open, the trapped middle ear air is absorbed, pressure falls below atmospheric, the drum retracts and the patient feels fullness with popping as the tube intermittently opens. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 57-year-old has a 3 cm mass at the carotid bifurcation that has enlarged slowly over two years. It is non-tender, moves laterally but not vertically, and a bruit is audible over it. On palpation the mass is pulsatile. Which investigation should be performed?","choices":["Computed tomography angiography","Fine needle aspiration","Plain radiography of the neck","Positron emission tomography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pulsatility indicates a vascular lesion, most likely a paraganglioma, and angiography defines the blood supply and the relationship to the carotid before anyone considers intervention. It is the study of choice for pulsatile masses, with magnetic resonance as a substitute that is generally less preferred. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 22-year-old with a peritonsillar abscess has trismus so severe the mouth opens barely two centimeters. What is the significance of the trismus?","choices":["Trismus indicates the abscess has spread to the mediastinum","Trismus is caused by vagus nerve involvement","Trismus is the most reliable symptom of this abscess","Trismus suggests an alternative diagnosis such as tetanus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pus tracking into the space adjacent to the medial pterygoid muscle irritates it and provokes spasm, which limits mouth opening. Of the classic triad of trismus, uvular deviation and dysphonia, trismus is the most reliable, and it is also what makes examination and drainage technically difficult. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman with sinusitis asks why her face hurts more when she leans forward to tie her shoes. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Bending compresses the nasal turbinates against the septum","Bending increases the hydrostatic pressure of the fluid within the sinus","Bending reduces venous drainage from the head","Bending triggers a trigeminal neuralgia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An obstructed sinus fills with fluid under pressure, and tilting the head forward raises the column of fluid pressing against the inflamed mucosa and its sensory nerve endings. That is why postural worsening is such a characteristic feature of sinusitis and helps separate it from other causes of facial pain. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with a thyroid carcinoma is found to have a solitary lesion in the femur. Which type of thyroid carcinoma is this most likely to be?","choices":["Anaplastic carcinoma","Follicular carcinoma","Medullary carcinoma","Papillary carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Follicular carcinoma is distinguished from papillary by its route of spread: it disseminates through the bloodstream to bone and lungs, whereas papillary tends to involve local cervical lymph nodes. A distant bony metastasis therefore points to the follicular type, and the Hurthle cell variant is more aggressive still. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has a slowly growing, painless submental swelling. Imaging shows it arises from the sublingual gland and extends through the mylohyoid muscle into the neck. What is the definitive treatment?","choices":["Aspiration of the cyst contents","Excision of the sublingual gland","Excision of the submandibular gland","Marsupialisation of the cyst"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A ranula is a mucous retention cyst from the sublingual gland, and it is called plunging when it herniates through mylohyoid into the neck. Removing the cyst alone leaves the gland that produces the mucus, so it simply refills; excising the source gland is what prevents recurrence. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man has a laceration of the auricle with exposed cartilage after a fall. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Debride and excise the exposed cartilage","Leave the wound open to heal by secondary intention","Prompt repair with a pressure dressing","Repair the skin only and omit the dressing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exposed auricular cartilage has no blood supply of its own and depends on the perichondrium, so leaving it uncovered leads to desiccation, infection and chondritis. Prompt repair restores that coverage, and the pressure dressing prevents a hematoma forming under the repaired perichondrium. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 36"}]};
